<h2>JavaScript Standard Style rules</h2>
<hr />
<p>
  These are a collection of the more "prevalent" style rules. For more detailed information and
  examples, please refer to
  <a href='https://standardjs.com/rules.html' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer'>
    JavaScript Standard Style's documentation
  </a>.<ul>
    <li>Use 2 spaces for indentation.</li>
    <li>
      Use 'single quotes' for strings except to avoid escaping e.g.
      <code>"It's"</code>
      instead of
      <code>'It\'s'</code>.
    </li>
    <li>Do not leave unused variables in code.</li>
    <li>
      Add a space after keywords e.g.
      <code>if (condition) { ... }</code>
      instead of
      <code>if(condition){ ... }</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Always use
      <code>===</code>
      instead of
      <code>==</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Infix operators must be spaced e.g.
      <code>1 + 1</code>
      instead of
      <code>1+1</code>.
    </li>
    <li>Commas should have a space after them.</li>
  </ul>
</p>
<h2>Markdown elements</h2>
<hr />
<p>
  For content written in Markdown, many elements are automatically targeted and styled with CSS.
</p>
<h3>## (h2)</h3>
<p>
  h2 elements are used for main titles of different topics. All h2 elements must be immediately
  followed by a horizontal rule (hr), and automatically have a "slide in" animation unless
  explicitly disabled.
</p>
<p
  class='heading'
  style='font-family: var(--plex-sans); font-size: var(--font-size-xxl); font-weight: var(--font-weight-bold);'
>
  Example of a h2
</p>
<h3>--- (hr)</h3>
<p>
  hr elements are used to give emphasis to h2 elements, and should only be used immediately after h2
  elements. These elements automatically have a "slide in" animation unless explicitly disabled.
  Example of a hr below:
</p>
<hr />
<h3>### (h3)</h3>
<p>
  h3 elements are used for smaller/sub-titles, such as different categories/points within a certain
  topic. h3 elements DO NOT require a hr after them.
</p>
<p
  style='font-family: var(--plex-sans); font-size: var(--font-size-l); font-weight: var(--font-weight-bold)'
>
  Example of a h3
</p>
<h3>Regular text (p)</h3>
<p>
  Regular text or text without any special elements are used for the content of the article and any
  attributions, such as this paragraph.
</p>
<p>They automatically have margin-bottom applied to them to separate paragraphs as shown here.</p>
<h3>- (ul item)</h3>
<ul style='margin-top: 0'>
  <li>
    Unordered lists are used when multiple things have to be listed and the order is not important,
    such as requirements to use a service.
  </li>
  <li>Styles such as margins are automatically handled.</li>
</ul>
<h3>1. 2. 3. ... (ol)</h3>
<ol style='margin-top: 0'>
  <li>
    Ordered lists are used when multiple things have to be listed in a specific order, such as steps
    to use a service.
  </li>
  <li>Styles such as margins are automatically handled.</li>
</ol>
<h3>![alt text](path) (img)</h3>
<p>
  Image elements are used for placing images in content. Use an absolute path (beginning with
  /assets/) to link to images, such as the one below. All images automatically have a "pop out"
  animation unless explicity disabled.
</p>
<img
  alt='plushie kingdom government ministers'
  src='/assets/news-media/introducing-the-plushie-kingdom-government/group-photo.webp'
/>
<h3>Links (a)</h3>
<p>
  For links, use the actual HTML syntax:
  <code>&lt;a href='link to stuff' class='link'&gt;LINK TEXT HERE&lt;/a&gt;</code>
  , as CSS classes cannot be added on to .md elements. Links without
  <code>class='link'</code>
  have their text-decoration (underline) removed, making them indistinguishable from regular text.
  For links to external content such as other websites, add
  <code>target='_blank' rel='noreferrer'</code>
  inside the link tag.
</p>
<p>
  <a class='link' href='https://github.com/J0e-Quan/pk-gov' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer'>
    Example of a link (opens pk-gov's GitHub repo)
  </a>
</p>
